WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.980
Would we even notice an attack?

00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:03.995
Four ways it stays invisible.

00:00:05.483 --> 00:00:08.993
First, the evidence was
deleted before anyone looked.

00:00:09.263 --> 00:00:13.753
Breaches often go unnoticed for weeks,
with a median of fourteen days.

00:00:14.098 --> 00:00:17.508
If logs were never kept,
or were erased after two weeks,

00:00:17.678 --> 00:00:19.508
the answer is "we do not know".

00:00:19.838 --> 00:00:23.268
Then every customer record
becomes possibly affected.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.860
Second, the alarm went off.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.560
Nobody was listening.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.860
A monitoring tool produces alerts,
not attention.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.810
The one that mattered, a new administrator
at 02:40 on a Saturday, is read on Monday.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.610
A weekend is enough to encrypt everything.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.620
Third, we watch where it is easy.

00:00:42.935 --> 00:00:47.045
Cloud accounts, software tools and
old controllers are often missing.

00:00:47.385 --> 00:00:51.815
Ask for the coverage map, and the honest
answer is often "we see about half".

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.100
The first step is a single page.

00:00:54.505 --> 00:00:58.215
Per system, watched or not,
and who signed off on the "not".

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.420
Fourth, detected on Friday,
handled on Monday.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.740
The alert becomes a ticket and waits.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:09.360
Nobody is sure they may pull a server
off the network on a Friday evening.

00:01:09.655 --> 00:01:12.275
Two decisions for this room:
someone on duty,

00:01:12.275 --> 00:01:15.065
and written permission
to act without asking.

00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:17.327
What good looks like.

00:01:17.612 --> 00:01:21.862
Central logs that an attacker cannot edit,
kept for at least a year.

00:01:22.102 --> 00:01:26.372
Ten written detections that matter,
with someone watching around the clock.

00:01:26.702 --> 00:01:28.152
A signed coverage map.

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:31.717
Playbooks, with standing
authority to isolate a machine.

00:01:32.032 --> 00:01:35.902
NIS2 Article 21 names incident handling,

00:01:36.272 --> 00:01:40.422
and you cannot report within
24 hours what you cannot detect.

00:01:42.297 --> 00:01:43.507
A suggested first step.

00:01:43.792 --> 00:01:45.802
At the next meeting, ask one thing.

00:01:46.097 --> 00:01:49.447
Last quarter,
how long on average to detect an attack?

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.292
If nobody can answer, that is the answer.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.040
Four ways an attack stays invisible.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.020
Measure how fast we notice.

